// Domain 4: Identity and Access Management (20%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d4-01", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 80 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and uses an external SAML 2.0 identity provider (IdP) for its 3,000 employees. Employees must sign in to AWS with their corporate credentials and the IdP's MFA. When an employee joins, changes teams, or leaves, the change must reach AWS within an hour without manual steps or scripts.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Change the IAM Identity Center identity source to the external IdP by using SAML 2.0.",
      "Turn on automatic provisioning in IAM Identity Center, and configure SCIM in the external IdP.",
      "Rely on IAM Identity Center to create each user automatically at the user's first SAML sign-in.",
      "Create an IAM SAML identity provider and federated roles in each of the 80 accounts.",
      "Keep the Identity Center directory as the source, and use the IdP only to check the passwords."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["With the external IdP as the identity source, employees sign in through the IdP with their corporate credentials and MFA, and get access to accounts in the organization.",
      "SCIM automatic provisioning lets the IdP create, update, and remove users and group memberships in IAM Identity Center as changes happen, with no manual steps.",
      "IAM Identity Center does not create users at their first sign-in. Users must be provisioned first, and leavers would never be removed.",
      "Federation set up separately in 80 accounts is far more work and still does not provision or remove users.",
      "The Identity Center directory cannot hand password checks to another IdP. The identity source is one or the other."] },

{ id: "d4-02", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS IAM Identity Center for 40 AWS accounts. Database administrators need the same permissions in the 12 accounts that run databases: an AWS managed policy and an inline policy that limits actions to resources tagged team=dba. When the security team changes the inline policy, the change must reach all 12 accounts without work in each account, and new administrators who join the group must get access automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create one permission set with the managed policy and the inline policy. Assign it to the administrators' group for the 12 accounts.",
      "Create one permission set with the managed policy and a customer managed policy reference. Create the referenced policy in each of the 12 accounts.",
      "Create one permission set with the managed policy and the inline policy. Assign it to each administrator user for the 12 accounts.",
      "Create one permission set for each of the 12 accounts with the managed policy and the inline policy. Assign each one to the group for its account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A permission set defines the policies once, and IAM Identity Center provisions the matching role in every account that it is assigned to. A change to the inline policy reaches all 12 accounts, and new group members get access automatically.",
      "A customer managed policy reference needs a policy with that name created and updated in every account, which is work in each account.",
      "Assignments to individual users do not cover administrators who join later.",
      "Twelve separate permission sets must each be updated when the inline policy changes."] },

{ id: "d4-03", d: 4,
  q: "A company is building a customer-facing web application for 500,000 customers. Customers must sign up and sign in with an email address and a password, and the company must require a second factor from an authenticator app for every customer. The company does not want to store passwords or build authentication code itself.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an Amazon Cognito user pool for sign-up and sign-in, and set MFA to required with TOTP software tokens.",
      "Use an Amazon Cognito user pool for sign-up and sign-in, and set MFA to optional with TOTP software tokens.",
      "Use an Amazon Cognito identity pool with unauthenticated identities, and require TOTP software tokens for each one.",
      "Add each customer to the IAM Identity Center directory, and set MFA to prompt every time they sign in."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Cognito user pool handles sign-up, sign-in, and password storage. Setting MFA to required with TOTP software tokens makes every customer use an authenticator app.",
      "Optional MFA lets customers skip the second factor, which does not meet the requirement for every customer.",
      "Identity pools give AWS credentials. They do not handle sign-up, passwords, or MFA, and unauthenticated identities have no sign-in at all.",
      "IAM Identity Center is for workforce access to AWS accounts and applications, not for half a million customers of a web application."] },

{ id: "d4-04", d: 4,
  q: "A mobile application signs users in with an Amazon Cognito user pool and has 2 million users. Each user must be able to upload files directly to an Amazon S3 bucket, but only under a prefix that belongs to that user. The application must not contain long-term AWS credentials, and no backend service may proxy the uploads.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use a Cognito identity pool for temporary credentials, with a role policy that allows s3:PutObject only on ${cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub}/*.",
      "Use a Cognito identity pool for temporary credentials, with a role policy that allows s3:PutObject on the whole bucket, and check the prefix in the app.",
      "Use a Cognito identity pool for temporary credentials, with a role policy that allows s3:PutObject only on the ${aws:username}/* prefix of the bucket.",
      "Create an IAM role for each user with a policy for that user's prefix, and have the app assume the role that matches the signed-in user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The identity pool exchanges the user pool token for temporary credentials. The cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub policy variable is unique to each identity, so one role policy limits every user to that user's own prefix.",
      "Every user would hold credentials for the whole bucket, and a check in the app can be bypassed.",
      "aws:username is set only for IAM users. It has no value for identities from a Cognito identity pool, so the policy would not match.",
      "Two million roles exceed IAM quotas, and the app would still need a way to get credentials for each role."] },

{ id: "d4-05", d: 4,
  q: "Servers in a company's data center run scripts that call AWS APIs with IAM user access keys stored on disk. The company already issues X.509 certificates to these servers from AWS Private Certificate Authority. The security team must replace the access keys with temporary credentials and must not run any new credential service.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an IAM Roles Anywhere trust anchor that uses the private certificate authority.",
      "Create a role that trusts rolesanywhere.amazonaws.com, and a Roles Anywhere profile with it.",
      "Create an IAM Roles Anywhere trust anchor from a self-signed certificate on each server.",
      "Register the servers with Systems Manager hybrid activation, and let the scripts use its role.",
      "Store the access keys in AWS Secrets Manager with rotation, and read them at script startup."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A trust anchor tells IAM Roles Anywhere which certificate authority to trust, so servers with certificates from the private CA can get temporary credentials.",
      "The role must trust the Roles Anywhere service principal, and a profile lists the roles that sessions can assume. The scripts then use the credential helper with each server's certificate.",
      "A trust anchor is the certificate authority, not one trust anchor per server, and self-signed certificates skip the company's existing CA.",
      "Hybrid activation credentials are meant for the SSM Agent, not for the server's own scripts.",
      "Rotated keys are still long-term access keys on the servers."] },

{ id: "d4-06", d: 4,
  q: "A company hires a third-party monitoring vendor that needs read-only access to its AWS account. The vendor's platform assumes roles from one vendor AWS account on behalf of hundreds of customers, and each customer enters its role ARN in the vendor's console. The company must prevent another customer of the vendor from making the vendor access the company's account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a role that trusts the vendor's account, with a condition that requires the unique external ID that the vendor assigned to the company.",
      "Create a role that trusts the vendor's account, with a condition that requires aws:PrincipalOrgID to equal the vendor's organization ID.",
      "Create a role that trusts the vendor's account, with a condition that requires aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent to be true for each request.",
      "Create a role that trusts the vendor's account with no conditions, and keep the role ARN secret from everyone except the vendor."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The vendor sends the external ID that it assigned to each customer when it assumes that customer's role. Another customer cannot make the vendor use the company's external ID, which prevents the confused deputy problem.",
      "Requests for every customer come from the same vendor organization, so this condition cannot tell them apart.",
      "The vendor's automated platform does not sign in with MFA, and MFA does not tell customers apart.",
      "Role ARNs are not secrets, so another customer could enter the company's ARN."] },

{ id: "d4-07", d: 4,
  q: "A company's 20 administrators use IAM users with access keys and console passwords. The security team must ensure that the administrators can stop or terminate Amazon EC2 instances only when they authenticated with MFA in the current session, including when they use the AWS CLI. Other EC2 actions must not require MFA.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a statement that denies ec2:StopInstances and ec2:TerminateInstances when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false, with the BoolIfExists operator.",
      "Add a statement that denies ec2:StopInstances and ec2:TerminateInstances when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false, with the Bool operator.",
      "Add a statement that denies all EC2 actions when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false, with the BoolIfExists operator for the key.",
      "Assign a virtual MFA device to each administrator, and keep the administrators' current IAM policies as they are."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Requests signed with long-term access keys do not include aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent. BoolIfExists treats a missing key as a match, so the deny applies to both non-MFA sessions and plain access keys.",
      "With the Bool operator, the deny does not apply when the key is missing, so CLI calls with plain access keys can still stop and terminate instances.",
      "Denying all EC2 actions makes MFA required for actions that must not need it.",
      "An MFA device alone does not make any policy require MFA."] },

{ id: "d4-08", d: 4,
  q: "A company must let an external auditor download one 2 GB report from a private Amazon S3 bucket. The auditor has no AWS account. Access must end automatically after 24 hours, the bucket must stay private, and the company does not want to create IAM users.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Generate a presigned URL for the object with a 24-hour expiration, signed with credentials that stay valid for at least 24 hours.",
      "Generate a presigned URL for the object with a 24-hour expiration, signed with an AWS Lambda function's execution role credentials.",
      "Add a bucket policy that allows s3:GetObject on the object to everyone, with an aws:CurrentTime condition that ends in 24 hours.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution with origin access control, and send the auditor the distribution URL for the object."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A presigned URL grants time-limited access to one object with the signer's permissions, and the bucket stays private. The URL works only while the signing credentials are valid, so they must last the full 24 hours.",
      "Lambda execution role credentials are temporary and usually expire long before 24 hours, which ends the URL early.",
      "A grant to everyone makes the object public for that day, and S3 Block Public Access would normally block it.",
      "The distribution URL has no expiration, so access would not end after 24 hours."] },

{ id: "d4-09", d: 4,
  q: "A developer generates Amazon S3 presigned URLs with a 7-day expiration from an AWS Lambda function by using the function's execution role. Customers report that the URLs stop working after a few hours. The bucket policy and the objects did not change.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The URLs were signed with the role's temporary credentials, and a presigned URL stops working when the credentials that signed it expire.",
      "Presigned URLs that are signed with Signature Version 4 cannot be valid for more than 12 hours, whatever expiration is set.",
      "The URL is tied to the Lambda invocation that created it, so it stops working some hours after that invocation ends.",
      "The bucket has S3 Bucket Keys turned on, which shortens the expiration of presigned URLs for objects encrypted with SSE-KMS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A presigned URL is valid only while the credentials that signed it are valid. Execution role credentials are temporary, so the URL stops working when they expire, even if the URL's own expiration is later.",
      "Signature Version 4 presigned URLs can be valid for up to 7 days when they are signed with long-term credentials.",
      "The URL depends on the signing credentials, not on the invocation.",
      "S3 Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not affect presigned URL expiration."] },

{ id: "d4-10", d: 4,
  q: "An application on Amazon EC2 reads messages from an Amazon SQS queue by using IAM user access keys that are stored in the AWS shared credentials file on the instance. A security review requires that the application uses only temporary credentials that are rotated automatically, with no credentials on disk. The application uses the AWS SDK default credential provider chain.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach an instance profile with a role that allows the SQS actions, and remove the access keys from the shared credentials file.",
      "Attach an instance profile with a role that allows the SQS actions, and keep the access keys in the credentials file as a fallback.",
      "Store the access keys in AWS Secrets Manager, and have the application read them at startup and keep them in memory.",
      "Store the access keys in the instance user data, and have the application read them from the instance metadata service."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The SDK gets the instance profile's temporary credentials from the metadata service and refreshes them automatically. Removing the keys from the file leaves no credentials on disk.",
      "The default provider chain checks the shared credentials file before the instance profile, so the application would keep using the long-term keys.",
      "The application would still use long-term access keys, only from a different place.",
      "User data is readable from the instance and is not for secrets, and the keys are still long-term."] },

{ id: "d4-11", d: 4,
  q: "A developer in account A has an identity policy that allows sts:AssumeRole on role R in account B. When the developer runs aws sts assume-role, the call fails with AccessDenied. Role R's trust policy names the account B root principal as its only principal, and no SCPs apply to either account.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update role R's trust policy to allow the developer's principal or account A.",
      "Attach a permissions policy to role R that allows sts:AssumeRole on role R itself, and keep its trust policy unchanged.",
      "Create an identity policy in account B that allows the developer's user ARN from account A to call sts:AssumeRole.",
      "Add the developer's IAM user from account A to a group in account B that has permission to assume role R."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account role assumption needs an allow on both sides. The developer's identity policy allows the call, but role R's trust policy trusts only account B, so it must also trust the developer or account A.",
      "A role's permissions policy controls what the role can do after it is assumed, not who can assume it.",
      "Identity policies in account B can be attached only to principals in account B.",
      "IAM groups can contain only users from their own account."] },

{ id: "d4-12", d: 4,
  q: "A company stores 5,000 workforce identities in an on-premises Microsoft Active Directory that is reachable from a VPC over AWS Direct Connect. Employees must sign in to AWS IAM Identity Center with their existing Active Directory credentials. The company does not want to copy users or password hashes into AWS.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AD Connector that points to the on-premises domain, and set it as the identity source for IAM Identity Center.",
      "Create an AWS Managed Microsoft AD directory, and synchronize the users and password hashes from the on-premises domain into the directory.",
      "Export the users from Active Directory, and import them into the IAM Identity Center directory from a CSV file once each week.",
      "Set up Simple AD in AWS Directory Service, and create each employee in Simple AD with the same user name as on premises."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AD Connector is a directory gateway that sends sign-in requests to the on-premises Active Directory without storing users or passwords in AWS. IAM Identity Center can use it as its identity source.",
      "Synchronizing users and password hashes copies them into AWS, which the company does not want.",
      "Importing users copies them into AWS, and their passwords would be separate from Active Directory.",
      "Simple AD is a separate directory, so the users and their passwords would be copied, not reused."] },

{ id: "d4-13", d: 4,
  q: "A user signs in to the AWS access portal through AWS IAM Identity Center but does not see the Production account. Other members of the same group see the account and its permission set. The user was added to the group in the external identity provider (IdP) yesterday, and the IdP's SCIM provisioning log shows a failed update for that user.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The user's new group membership has not been provisioned to IAM Identity Center because the SCIM update failed.",
      "The group's permission set was never provisioned to the Production account, so no member of the group can use it.",
      "An SCP in the Production account denies the user's sessions, which also hides the account from the user in the portal.",
      "The user has not registered an MFA device in IAM Identity Center, which hides all accounts until a device is registered."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access comes from the group's permission set assignment, which works for the other members. The failed SCIM update means that IAM Identity Center never received the user's new group membership.",
      "Other members of the group see the account, so the permission set is provisioned there.",
      "SCPs limit permissions after sign-in. They do not change which accounts the portal lists.",
      "Missing MFA affects the sign-in itself, not which accounts are listed after sign-in."] },

{ id: "d4-14", d: 4,
  q: "A company's mobile app uses an Amazon Cognito user pool and an identity pool. Users in the user pool's Admins group must receive an IAM role with write access to the app's data, and all other signed-in users must receive a read-only role. Users must not be able to get a role that their group does not allow.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Assign an IAM role to each user pool group, and configure the identity pool to choose the role from the token.",
      "Assign an IAM role to each user pool group, and configure the identity pool to use the default authenticated role.",
      "Create two identity pools, and have the app choose the pool from the group claim in each user's ID token.",
      "Give every signed-in user the write role, and check the Admins group claim in the app before each write."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["User pool groups can carry IAM roles. When the identity pool chooses the role from the token, it uses the roles in the user's token, so admins get the write role and other users get the read-only role.",
      "With the default authenticated role, every user gets the same role, so admins would not get write access.",
      "The app decides which pool to use, and a modified app could pick the admin pool.",
      "Every user would hold write credentials, and a check in the app can be bypassed."] },

{ id: "d4-15", d: 4,
  q: "A REST API in Amazon API Gateway serves a web application whose users sign in with an Amazon Cognito user pool. The API must reject requests that do not carry a valid, unexpired token from that user pool before the backend is called. The company does not want to write or run custom authorization code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a Cognito user pool authorizer, and set it on each method of the API.",
      "Create a Lambda authorizer that validates the user pool tokens, and set it on each method.",
      "Set IAM authorization on each method, and send the users' tokens to the backend to check.",
      "Require an API key on each method, and give each user an API key after the user signs in."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Cognito user pool authorizer validates tokens from the user pool, including their signature and expiration, and API Gateway rejects requests without a valid token before calling the backend.",
      "A Lambda authorizer works, but it is the custom code that the company does not want.",
      "IAM authorization requires requests signed with AWS credentials, not user pool tokens, and the backend check is custom code.",
      "API keys identify callers for usage plans. They are not authentication."] },

{ id: "d4-16", d: 4,
  q: "A deployment script on an Amazon EC2 instance uses the instance role to assume role A, and then uses role A's credentials to assume role B in another account with a requested duration of 4 hours. Role B's maximum session duration is 12 hours. The second AssumeRole call fails with a validation error about the duration.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["Role chaining limits the session to 1 hour, so a request for 4 hours fails when the caller uses role A's session credentials.",
      "Role B's maximum session duration must be raised above 12 hours before any caller can request a session of 4 hours.",
      "The instance role's credentials expire after 6 hours, so every role session that starts from them must be shorter than 1 hour.",
      "Cross-account role sessions are limited to 1 hour unless the role's trust policy sets the sts:DurationSeconds condition key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Assuming a role with another role's credentials is role chaining, which caps the session at 1 hour whatever the role's maximum session duration is.",
      "A 4-hour request is within role B's 12-hour maximum, so the maximum is not the problem.",
      "The cap comes from role chaining, not from the lifetime of the instance role's credentials.",
      "The sts:DurationSeconds condition key can restrict durations. It cannot lift the role chaining limit."] },

{ id: "d4-17", d: 4,
  q: "Employees federate from a SAML identity provider (IdP) into a shared role, and some employees then assume other roles from that role. The security team must identify in AWS CloudTrail which employee performed each action, including actions taken after the employees assume other roles. Employees must not be able to change that identity.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Configure the IdP to send each employee's user name in the SourceIdentity SAML attribute at federation.",
      "Allow sts:SetSourceIdentity in the trust policies of the shared role and the roles assumed from it.",
      "Ask the employees to put their user names in the RoleSessionName each time they assume another role.",
      "Send each employee's user name as a transitive session tag, and look it up in the AssumeRole events.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail Insights for the trail, which adds the federated user name to every event."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The IdP can set a source identity at federation. Once set, it cannot be changed in that session or in role sessions that start from it.",
      "Setting a source identity requires sts:SetSourceIdentity in the trust policy of each role that receives it. CloudTrail then records the source identity on every action.",
      "Employees choose the session name and can change it in each new session.",
      "Session tags are recorded in the AssumeRole events, not on each later action, so every action would need a manual lookup.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual call rates. It does not add identity information to events."] },

{ id: "d4-18", d: 4,
  q: "Pods in an Amazon EKS cluster on managed node groups use the node's instance role, so every pod has the same AWS permissions. Each of the 12 applications must have only its own AWS permissions through temporary credentials. Pods must not be able to use the node's instance role credentials.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Install the EKS Pod Identity Agent, and create a Pod Identity association for each application's service account and role.",
      "Require IMDSv2 on the nodes with a hop limit of 1, which stops pods from reaching the node's instance role credentials.",
      "Add each application's permissions to the node instance role, and restrict the pods with Kubernetes RBAC rules.",
      "Store an IAM user's access keys for each application in a Kubernetes secret, and mount the secret in its pods.",
      "Set the IMDSv2 hop limit on the nodes to 3, and keep IMDSv1 enabled for the pods that still need it."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["EKS Pod Identity gives each pod temporary credentials for the role that is associated with its service account, so each application gets only its own permissions.",
      "Pods are one network hop further from the metadata service than the node. With IMDSv2 and a hop limit of 1, pods cannot get the node role's credentials.",
      "Kubernetes RBAC controls the Kubernetes API, not AWS API calls, so every pod would still have every permission.",
      "Access keys in secrets are long-term credentials.",
      "A higher hop limit and IMDSv1 let pods reach the node's credentials."] },

{ id: "d4-19", d: 4,
  q: "A company manages access to 15 Amazon EKS clusters by editing the aws-auth ConfigMap in each cluster. A bad edit recently locked all administrators out of one cluster. The company wants to grant and audit IAM principals' access to the Kubernetes API through the Amazon EKS API and AWS CloudTrail instead of editing the ConfigMap.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Change each cluster's authentication mode to the EKS API, and create access entries with access policies for the principals.",
      "Change each cluster's authentication mode to the EKS API and ConfigMap, and keep managing access through the aws-auth ConfigMap.",
      "Keep the aws-auth ConfigMap, and give every administrator the IAM role that created each cluster as a fallback for lockouts.",
      "Create EKS Pod Identity associations for the administrators' IAM roles in each cluster, and remove them from the ConfigMap."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access entries are managed through the EKS API, so changes are recorded in CloudTrail, and EKS access policies grant Kubernetes permissions without the ConfigMap.",
      "The combined mode still allows the ConfigMap, and continuing to edit it keeps the risk that caused the lockout.",
      "Handing out the cluster creator's role gives everyone full access and does not change how access is managed.",
      "Pod Identity gives pods AWS credentials. It does not grant people access to the Kubernetes API."] },

{ id: "d4-20", d: 4,
  q: "Users of a mobile app sign in with an Amazon Cognito user pool, and the app exchanges their tokens through a Cognito identity pool for AWS credentials. After the team moved the app to a new identity pool in the same account, every AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity call for the authenticated role fails. The role and its permissions were not changed.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The role's trust policy has a cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:aud condition that still contains the old identity pool ID.",
      "The role's trust policy has a cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:amr condition that still allows only unauthenticated identities.",
      "The role's permissions policy does not include cognito-identity:GetCredentialsForIdentity for the new identity pool.",
      "The new identity pool must be in a different Region from the user pool before it can issue credentials for the role."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Roles for an identity pool normally trust only that pool through an aud condition with the pool ID. The condition still names the old pool, so tokens from the new pool are rejected.",
      "The role worked for authenticated users before the move, so its amr condition already allowed them.",
      "A role's permissions policy does not decide who can assume the role. The trust policy does.",
      "An identity pool and a user pool can be in the same Region."] },

{ id: "d4-21", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses the IAM Identity Center directory as its identity source for 600 users. A new policy requires every user to enter an MFA code each time they sign in to the AWS access portal. Users who have no MFA device yet must be required to register one at their next sign-in.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Set the MFA prompt to every time they sign in (always-on), and require users without a device to register one at sign-in.",
      "Set the MFA prompt to only when the sign-in context changes, and require users without a device to register one at sign-in.",
      "Set the MFA prompt to every time they sign in (always-on), and send users without a device a one-time password by email.",
      "Add a statement to every permission set that denies all actions when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false for the session."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The always-on setting prompts for MFA at every sign-in, and the setting for users without a device can require them to register one at sign-in.",
      "Context-aware prompts appear only when the sign-in context looks different, not at every sign-in.",
      "An emailed one-time password lets users sign in without ever registering a device.",
      "Permission set policies apply after sign-in. They do not control the portal's MFA prompt."] },

{ id: "d4-22", d: 4,
  q: "A company's GitHub Actions workflows deploy to AWS by using IAM user access keys that are stored as repository secrets. The company must remove the long-term keys. Only workflows that run on the main branch of one specific repository may assume the deployment role, and workflows in other repositories of the same GitHub organization must be denied.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
  o: ["Create an IAM OIDC identity provider for token.actions.githubusercontent.com with the audience sts.amazonaws.com.",
      "Add trust policy conditions on the token's aud for sts.amazonaws.com and its sub for the repository's main branch.",
      "Give the workflow the id-token write permission, and use the configure-aws-credentials action to assume the role.",
      "Add a trust policy condition that checks only the token's sub for the organization name with a repository wildcard.",
      "Rotate the access keys every 7 days with a scheduled Lambda function, and update the repository secrets each time."],
  a: [0, 1, 2],
  r: ["An OIDC identity provider lets IAM trust tokens from GitHub, so workflows can exchange them for temporary credentials.",
      "Conditions on aud and on sub, which contains the repository and the branch, limit the role to workflows on the main branch of that one repository.",
      "The workflow needs permission to request an OIDC token, and the action exchanges it for role credentials, so no keys are stored.",
      "A wildcard for every repository in the organization lets the other repositories assume the role.",
      "Rotated keys are still long-term credentials stored in GitHub."] },

{ id: "d4-23", d: 4,
  q: "A developer's AWS CLI calls to assume a role fail with AccessDenied. The role's trust policy allows the developer's account only when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is true. The developer has a registered virtual MFA device and calls aws sts assume-role with long-term access keys from a named CLI profile.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Have the developer pass --serial-number and --token-code with a current MFA code in the assume-role call.",
      "Have the developer run aws sts get-caller-identity first, which refreshes the CLI session with the MFA context.",
      "Change the trust policy condition to aws:MultiFactorAuthAge less than 3600, and remove the other MFA condition.",
      "Attach the IAMFullAccess managed policy to the developer, and have the developer call aws sts assume-role again."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Requests signed with long-term access keys carry no MFA context. When the developer passes the MFA serial number and a current code, AWS STS verifies the code, and the request meets the condition.",
      "get-caller-identity does not add MFA context to later calls.",
      "Requests without MFA have no aws:MultiFactorAuthAge value, so the call would still fail.",
      "No permissions policy can satisfy a condition in the role's trust policy."] },

{ id: "d4-24", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 30 project teams, and new teams start every month. Each team must be able to start and stop only the Amazon EC2 instances that belong to its own project, including instances launched later. Engineers sign in through AWS IAM Identity Center, which passes a project attribute, and every instance has a project tag. No policy may need to change when a team or an instance is added.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use one policy that allows ec2:StartInstances and ec2:StopInstances when aws:ResourceTag/project is ${aws:PrincipalTag/project}.",
      "Use one policy that allows ec2:StartInstances and ec2:StopInstances when aws:RequestTag/project equals ${aws:PrincipalTag/project}.",
      "Create a policy for each team that allows ec2:StartInstances and ec2:StopInstances on the ARNs of that team's instances.",
      "Use one policy that allows ec2:StartInstances and ec2:StopInstances when the aws:ResourceTag/project key exists on the instance."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attribute-based access control compares the tag on the instance with the principal's own project tag. One policy covers every team and instance, including new ones, as long as the tags are set.",
      "aws:RequestTag holds tags that are passed in the request, such as when creating or tagging a resource. Start and stop requests carry no tags, so the condition never matches.",
      "Policies with instance ARNs must change every time a team or an instance is added.",
      "Checking only that the tag exists lets every engineer start and stop every project's instances."] },

{ id: "d4-25", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS IAM Identity Center with an external identity provider (IdP) that stores each employee's cost center. One permission set must let employees manage only the resources whose CostCenter tag matches their own cost center. The company has 120 cost centers and does not want a permission set or a group for each one.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on attributes for access control, map the IdP's cost center attribute to the CostCenter key, and compare aws:PrincipalTag/CostCenter with resource tags in the permission set.",
      "Turn on attributes for access control, map the IdP's cost center attribute to the CostCenter key, and compare aws:RequestTag/CostCenter with the resource tags in the permission set.",
      "Create a group for each cost center in the IdP, and assign one permission set to all 120 groups with a condition that matches the group name to the resource tag.",
      "Add the cost center to each employee's user name in the IdP, and match it to the resource tags in the permission set with the aws:username condition key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attributes for access control pass user attributes from the identity source as session tags. The permission set's policy can then compare aws:PrincipalTag/CostCenter with resource tags, so one permission set serves all 120 cost centers.",
      "The cost center arrives as a principal tag on the session, not as a tag in the request, so aws:RequestTag does not hold it.",
      "This needs 120 groups, and IAM policies have no condition key for an Identity Center group name.",
      "aws:username is set for IAM users only, not for IAM Identity Center sessions."] },

{ id: "d4-26", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants developers to create IAM roles for their own AWS Lambda functions without help from the security team. Every role that the developers create must be limited by an approved permissions boundary policy. The developers must not be able to weaken or remove that limit, including by editing the boundary policy itself.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Allow iam:CreateRole only when the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition key equals the approved boundary policy ARN.",
      "Deny changes to the boundary policy's versions, and deny iam:DeleteRolePermissionsBoundary to the developers.",
      "Attach the approved boundary to each developer's own IAM user, and allow iam:CreateRole with no conditions.",
      "Allow iam:CreateRole only when the role name starts with the developer's user name, and review new roles weekly.",
      "Allow iam:PutRolePermissionsBoundary on all roles, and ask developers to set the boundary after each role is created."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The condition key makes the boundary mandatory, so every role that a developer creates is limited by the approved boundary.",
      "Without these denies, a developer could add a new version of the boundary policy or remove the boundary from a role, which undoes the limit.",
      "A boundary on the developer limits the developer, not the roles that the developer creates, which could still get any permissions.",
      "Naming rules and weekly reviews do not limit what the roles can do.",
      "Setting the boundary afterward is optional, so roles can exist without it."] },

{ id: "d4-27", d: 4,
  q: "A multi-tenant SaaS application assumes one IAM role to read tenant data from an Amazon S3 bucket that has one prefix for each of 3,000 tenants. The company wants each request to be limited to the current tenant's prefix even if the application code has a bug, without creating a role for each tenant.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Pass a session policy in each AssumeRole call that allows s3:GetObject only on the current tenant's prefix.",
      "Pass a session tag with the tenant ID in each AssumeRole call, and keep the role's policy allowing the whole bucket.",
      "Grant the role s3:GetObject on all prefixes, and check the tenant ID in the application before each read.",
      "Create an S3 access point for the role, and allow s3:GetObject on the whole bucket in the access point policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A session's permissions are the intersection of the role's policies and the session policy. A session policy for the current tenant limits that session to the tenant's prefix, whatever the code tries to read.",
      "A session tag limits nothing unless the role's policy uses it. A policy that still allows the whole bucket gives every session every tenant's data.",
      "A check in code is the control that a bug could break.",
      "An access point policy for the whole bucket gives the same access to every tenant's prefix."] },

{ id: "d4-28", d: 4,
  q: "A role in account B must read objects from an Amazon S3 bucket in account A. The objects are encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key in account A. The bucket policy allows the role to call s3:GetObject, but the role receives AccessDenied on every GetObject call.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Update the key policy in account A to allow the role in account B to use kms:Decrypt with the key.",
      "Add kms:Decrypt on the key's ARN to the identity policy of the role in account B.",
      "Add a statement to the bucket policy that allows the role to use kms:Decrypt on the key.",
      "Create a grant on the key from account B for the role, and keep the key policy unchanged.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3, which re-encrypts the existing objects."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Cross-account use of a KMS key needs the key policy in the key's account to allow the other account or its principal.",
      "The role also needs an identity policy in its own account that allows kms:Decrypt on the key. Both sides must allow the action.",
      "A bucket policy controls S3 access. It cannot grant permissions on a KMS key.",
      "Account B cannot create grants on a key that it does not have permission to use, so the grant would have to come from account A.",
      "Default encryption applies only to new objects. Existing objects keep their KMS key."] },

{ id: "d4-29", d: 4,
  q: "An administrator in a member account of an organization in AWS Organizations has the AdministratorAccess policy and no other policies. Every call that the administrator makes in eu-west-3 fails with an explicit deny, while the same calls in us-east-1 succeed. AWS CloudTrail shows that the account's IAM policies did not change.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["An SCP on the account denies actions when aws:RequestedRegion is not one of the approved Regions.",
      "eu-west-3 is an opt-in Region that has not been enabled for the account, so every call there is denied.",
      "A permissions boundary on the administrator's role leaves out eu-west-3, which produces an explicit deny.",
      "An RCP attached to the account denies every request that the administrator makes in eu-west-3."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SCPs set the maximum permissions in member accounts. An SCP that denies requests outside approved Regions produces an explicit deny even for administrators whose IAM policies allow everything.",
      "eu-west-3 is enabled by default, and calls to a disabled Region fail with a different error, not an explicit deny.",
      "The administrator has no boundary, and an action that a boundary leaves out is an implicit deny, not an explicit one.",
      "RCPs apply only to resources of a few services, so an RCP cannot deny every call in a Region."] },

{ id: "d4-30", d: 4,
  q: "A security engineer has written a new IAM policy for a group of 40 analysts. Before the policy is attached, the engineer must confirm which Amazon S3 actions it allows on two specific buckets when it is combined with the permissions boundary that the analysts already have. The engineer must also see which statement decides each result.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use the IAM Policy Simulator with the new policy, the existing permissions boundary, the S3 actions, and the two bucket ARNs.",
      "Run IAM Access Analyzer policy validation on the new policy, and read its findings to see which S3 actions are allowed.",
      "Run IAM Access Analyzer policy generation on the analysts' group, and compare the generated policy with the new one.",
      "Attach the policy to a test user with the same boundary, and call each S3 action against the buckets to see which fail."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The Policy Simulator evaluates identity policies and permissions boundaries against chosen actions and resources without real requests, and it shows which statement allows or denies each action.",
      "Policy validation checks grammar and best practices. It does not report which actions the policy allows on specific buckets.",
      "Policy generation builds a policy from past activity. It does not evaluate a new policy.",
      "Real calls against the buckets are risky and do not show which statement made each decision."] },

{ id: "d4-31", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 300 accounts in AWS Organizations. The security team must continuously find every S3 bucket, KMS key, IAM role, Secrets Manager secret, and other supported resource whose policy grants access to principals outside the organization, including resources that are created later.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust, from a delegated administrator account.",
      "Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer with the account as the zone of trust in each account in the organization.",
      "Create an IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer for the organization, and review its findings for principals outside it.",
      "Use the IAM Policy Simulator in each account to test every resource policy against a list of known outside accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust continuously finds supported resources, including new ones, that grant access to principals outside the organization.",
      "With each account as its own zone of trust, access between the company's own accounts also creates findings, which buries the outside access.",
      "Unused access analyzers report unused roles, keys, and permissions, not access granted to outside principals.",
      "The simulator tests one policy at a time and cannot find unknown outside principals."] },

{ id: "d4-32", d: 4,
  q: "A security team must find IAM roles and users that have not been used in 90 days, access keys that have not been used, and permissions that roles have but never use, across all 120 accounts in its organization. The results must stay current without scripts.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer for the organization with a 90-day tracking period, from a delegated administrator account.",
      "Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer for the organization, and review its findings for unused roles and access keys.",
      "Download the IAM credential report from each account each month, and compare the last used dates in a spreadsheet for all accounts.",
      "Review the last accessed information for each role in the IAM console of each account every 90 days, and record the unused services."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unused access analyzer continuously reports unused roles, users, access keys, and permissions across the organization, based on the tracking period that is set.",
      "External access analyzers report access from outside the zone of trust, not unused access.",
      "Credential reports are per account and do not show permissions that roles never use.",
      "Reviewing roles by hand in 120 accounts is manual work that is out of date between reviews."] },

{ id: "d4-33", d: 4,
  q: "An application team's IAM role has broad permissions that were granted during development. The application has run in production for 60 days, and AWS CloudTrail logs all management events for the account. The security team must replace the broad policy with one that allows only the actions and services that the application actually used.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate a policy from the role's CloudTrail activity for the last 60 days, then review and attach it.",
      "Run IAM Access Analyzer policy validation on the broad policy, and remove the statements that the validation reports as warnings.",
      "Use the role's last accessed information to remove unused services, and keep every action for the services that the role used.",
      "Replace the broad policy with the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy, and add write actions back when the application fails."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Policy generation reads the role's CloudTrail activity for the chosen period and creates a policy with the actions and services that were used, which the team reviews before attaching.",
      "Validation warnings are about policy quality, not about which actions the application used.",
      "Last accessed information works at the service level here, so every unused action in a used service would stay.",
      "Starting from ReadOnlyAccess breaks production while write actions are added back, and it still allows reads the application never makes."] },

{ id: "d4-34", d: 4,
  q: "Developers change IAM policies as JSON files in a CI/CD pipeline. The security team wants the pipeline to fail automatically before deployment when a changed policy grants any access that the currently deployed version does not grant, without people reading each change.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add an IAM Access Analyzer custom policy check that runs CheckNoNewAccess with the deployed version as the reference policy.",
      "Add an IAM Access Analyzer custom policy check that runs CheckAccessNotGranted with a list of the actions that the policy uses today.",
      "Add an IAM Access Analyzer policy validation step, and fail the pipeline on any security warning that it reports for the changed policy.",
      "Run the IAM Policy Simulator after deployment for every action, and roll the deployment back when the simulator finds any new access."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CheckNoNewAccess compares a new policy with a reference policy and fails when the new policy grants access that the reference does not. With the deployed version as the reference, the pipeline stops before deployment.",
      "CheckAccessNotGranted checks that specific actions are not granted, so it cannot detect new access in general.",
      "Validation reports policy errors and warnings. It does not compare the changed policy with the deployed one.",
      "Checking after deployment lets the new access exist in production first."] },

{ id: "d4-35", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket stores software packages that every account in the company's organization must be able to read, including accounts that are created later. The bucket must not be readable by accounts outside the organization, and the security team does not want to update the bucket policy when accounts are added.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:GetObject for all principals when aws:PrincipalOrgID equals the organization ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:GetObject for all principals when aws:ResourceOrgID equals the organization ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:GetObject for the root principal of each account that is in the organization.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that allows s3:GetObject for all principals when aws:PrincipalAccount is the management account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:PrincipalOrgID is the organization of the caller's account. One statement allows every current and future account in the organization and no account outside it.",
      "aws:ResourceOrgID is the organization of the bucket's own account, which is always the company's, so every caller in the world would match.",
      "Listing each account's root principal requires a policy change for every new account.",
      "This allows only the management account, not the other accounts in the organization."] },

{ id: "d4-36", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon SNS topic receives event notifications from an Amazon S3 bucket named reports-prod in the same account. The topic policy allows the s3.amazonaws.com service principal to publish with no conditions. The security team must ensure that only that bucket, owned by the company's account, can cause Amazon S3 to publish to the topic.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add aws:SourceArn for the bucket's ARN and aws:SourceAccount for the company's account to the statement for the service principal.",
      "Add aws:PrincipalArn for the bucket's ARN to the statement for the service principal, and keep the other settings of the statement.",
      "Add aws:PrincipalOrgID for the company's organization ID to the statement for the service principal, and keep the other settings.",
      "Replace the service principal in the statement with the root principal of the company's account, and keep the other settings."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When a service principal is allowed, aws:SourceArn and aws:SourceAccount limit access to requests that the service makes on behalf of the named resource and account, which prevents the cross-service confused deputy problem.",
      "Requests from a service principal do not carry the bucket's ARN as aws:PrincipalArn.",
      "A service principal does not belong to an organization, so aws:PrincipalOrgID does not match its requests.",
      "Amazon S3 publishes as the service principal, not as the account, so notifications would stop."] },

{ id: "d4-37", d: 4,
  q: "A company is building a document management application with 50,000 users. Rules such as which users can view, edit, or share each document depend on the user's role and on attributes of each document. The rules must be kept outside the application code, written in a policy language that auditors can read, and changed without redeploying the application.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use Amazon Verified Permissions with a policy store and Cedar policies, and call it for an authorization decision on each request.",
      "Use Amazon Cognito user pool groups for the roles, and check the document attributes in the application code on each request.",
      "Create an IAM role for each document with the allowed actions, and have the application assume the matching role on each request.",
      "Store the rules as AWS AppConfig feature flags, and evaluate the flags and document attributes in the application code on each request."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Verified Permissions stores fine-grained authorization rules as Cedar policies outside the application. The application asks for a decision on each request, and policies change without a redeployment.",
      "The document attribute checks would still be logic in the application code.",
      "Roles for documents do not scale and are not meant to model application permissions.",
      "Feature flags hold values, so the authorization logic would still live in the application code."] },

{ id: "d4-38", d: 4,
  q: "Team A's lead must be able to create, update, and delete IAM roles for team A's applications, but must not be able to change roles that belong to the other 12 teams in the account. All of team A's roles are created under the /team-a/ path, and team A creates new roles every week.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Allow the IAM role actions only on the resource arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/team-a/*.",
      "Allow the IAM role actions only on the resource arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/team-a*.",
      "Allow the IAM role actions only on the ARNs of team A's current roles, and update the list weekly.",
      "Allow the IAM role actions on all roles, and deny them when aws:RequestedRegion is not team A's Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A role's path is part of its ARN. Allowing the actions only on role/team-a/* covers every current and future role under the /team-a/ path and nothing else.",
      "Without the slash after team-a, the pattern also matches other roles whose names or paths start with team-a, such as a team-admin role.",
      "A fixed list must be updated every week as team A creates roles.",
      "IAM is a global service, so a Region condition does not separate one team's roles from another's."] },

{ id: "d4-39", d: 4,
  q: "Developers launch Amazon EC2 instances for their applications and attach an instance profile at launch. They must be able to pass only the app-instance role, and only to Amazon EC2. They must not be able to pass any other role, such as an administrator role.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Allow iam:PassRole only on the app-instance role's ARN, with iam:PassedToService set to ec2.amazonaws.com.",
      "Allow iam:PassRole on all roles, with the iam:PassedToService condition set to ec2.amazonaws.com.",
      "Allow iam:PassRole only on the app-instance role's ARN, with no condition on the service it is passed to.",
      "Allow ec2:AssociateIamInstanceProfile on all instances, and allow iam:AttachRolePolicy on the app-instance role."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attaching a role to an instance requires iam:PassRole on that role. Limiting the resource to the app-instance role and the condition to the EC2 service allows exactly that.",
      "Any role, including an administrator role, could be passed to EC2.",
      "Without the service condition, the developers could pass the app-instance role to services other than EC2.",
      "These permissions do not control which role is passed, and AttachRolePolicy lets the developers change the role's permissions."] },

{ id: "d4-40", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon EC2 RunInstances call fails with an UnauthorizedOperation error that includes an encoded authorization message. The security engineer must see why the request was denied, including whether an SCP, a permissions boundary, or an identity policy caused it, and which conditions did not match.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Call sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage on the encoded message as a principal that is allowed to call it.",
      "Call kms:Decrypt on the encoded message by using the AWS managed key for Amazon EC2 in the account.",
      "Find the RunInstances event in AWS CloudTrail, where the errorMessage field shows the reasons in clear text.",
      "Paste the encoded message into the IAM Policy Simulator, and run a simulation for ec2:RunInstances."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DecodeAuthorizationMessage returns the details of the denied request, such as the policies and conditions that were evaluated. The caller needs permission for sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage.",
      "The message is not encrypted with a KMS key, so KMS cannot decode it.",
      "The CloudTrail event contains the same encoded message, not the decoded reasons.",
      "The Policy Simulator does not accept encoded authorization messages."] },

{ id: "d4-41", d: 4,
  q: "An AWS Lambda function in account A must be invoked by an application role in account B. The role in account B has an identity policy that allows lambda:InvokeFunction on the function's ARN, but every call fails with AccessDenied. No SCPs apply, and the function has no resource-based policy.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Add a statement to the function's resource-based policy that allows account B's role to call lambda:InvokeFunction.",
      "Add lambda:InvokeFunction for the role in account B to the permissions of the function's execution role in account A.",
      "Add a trust policy statement to the function's execution role that allows the role in account B to assume the role.",
      "Add lambda:InvokeFunctionUrl on the function to the identity policy of the role in account B, and keep the rest as is."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account invocation needs a resource-based policy on the function that allows the caller. Both the identity policy in account B and the function's resource-based policy must allow the call.",
      "The execution role controls what the function can do, not who can invoke it.",
      "Assuming the execution role does not give the right to invoke the function, and it exposes the function's own permissions.",
      "InvokeFunctionUrl applies to function URLs, and the function still has no policy that allows account B."] },

{ id: "d4-42", d: 4,
  q: "A shared Amazon S3 data lake bucket is used by 25 teams, and its bucket policy is close to the 20 KB size limit and hard to review. Each team must have its own access rules for its own prefix, managed separately by the data platform team, and all teams must keep using the same bucket.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an S3 access point for each team with its own access point policy, and delegate access control from the bucket policy to the access points.",
      "Create an S3 access point for each team with its own access point policy, and keep a copy of every team's rules in the bucket policy too.",
      "Create a separate bucket for each team, and replicate the shared data into all 25 of the team buckets by using S3 Replication rules.",
      "Split the bucket policy into 25 smaller policies, and attach one bucket policy for each team to the same bucket in the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Each access point has its own policy for one team's prefix. When the bucket policy delegates access control to the access points, the team rules leave the bucket policy and are managed separately.",
      "Keeping every team's rules in the bucket policy as well leaves it at the size limit and just as hard to review.",
      "Separate buckets break the requirement to keep using one bucket and add replication cost.",
      "A bucket can have only one bucket policy."] },

{ id: "d4-43", d: 4,
  q: "A developer's identity policy allows sqs:SendMessage on a queue in the same account. The developer's IAM user also has a permissions boundary that allows only Amazon S3 and Amazon EC2 actions. The queue's access policy does not mention the developer, and every call to send a message fails with AccessDenied.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The permissions boundary does not allow sqs:SendMessage, so the developer's request is not allowed.",
      "The identity policy must use a wildcard in the queue ARN, because SQS ignores exact queue ARNs in policies.",
      "Permissions boundaries apply only to IAM roles, so a boundary on an IAM user blocks all of the user's actions.",
      "The developer must authenticate with MFA before any SQS action is allowed for an IAM user in the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With a permissions boundary, a request is allowed only when both the identity policy and the boundary allow it. The boundary leaves out SQS, so sending is implicitly denied.",
      "SQS supports exact queue ARNs in policies.",
      "Permissions boundaries apply to IAM users and roles, and they limit only the actions they leave out.",
      "Nothing in the policies requires MFA."] },

{ id: "d4-44", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket policy denies all S3 actions when aws:SourceVpce does not match the company's VPC endpoint. An administrator who has the AdministratorAccess policy cannot list the bucket's objects in the AWS Management Console from the office network, but applications in the VPC can read the objects.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The console request does not come through the VPC endpoint, so the bucket policy's explicit deny applies to it.",
      "The AdministratorAccess policy does not include s3:ListBucket, which the console needs to list the objects in the bucket.",
      "An implicit deny applies, because the bucket policy does not name the administrator's role in any statement that allows access.",
      "The console uses an S3 control plane that ignores bucket policies, so an SCP must be denying the administrator's request."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An explicit deny in any policy overrides every allow. Requests from the office browser do not use the VPC endpoint, so the deny matches and blocks them.",
      "AdministratorAccess allows all S3 actions.",
      "In the same account, the identity policy's allow is enough without the bucket policy naming the role. The explicit deny is what blocks the request.",
      "Console requests are normal S3 requests, so the bucket policy applies to them."] },

{ id: "d4-45", d: 4,
  q: "An application role in account B must read a secret in AWS Secrets Manager in account A. The secret is encrypted with the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager. The role's identity policy already allows secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on the secret's ARN and kms:Decrypt on all keys in account A.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Re-encrypt the secret with a customer managed key whose key policy allows the role to use kms:Decrypt.",
      "Attach a resource-based policy to the secret that allows the role in account B to call GetSecretValue.",
      "Add the role in account B to the key policy of the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager in account A.",
      "Share the secret with account B by using AWS RAM, and keep the AWS managed key for the secret.",
      "Replicate the secret to account B with Secrets Manager replication, and keep the AWS managed key."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The policy of an AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account, so the secret must use a customer managed key whose policy allows the role.",
      "Cross-account access to a secret also needs a resource-based policy on the secret that allows the role in the other account.",
      "AWS managed key policies cannot be edited.",
      "Cross-account access to secrets uses resource-based policies, not AWS RAM, and the AWS managed key would still block decryption.",
      "Secrets Manager replication copies secrets to other Regions in the same account, not to other accounts."] },

{ id: "d4-46", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses ABAC. Policies allow principals to manage Amazon EC2 instances when the instance's project tag matches the principal's project tag. An audit found that some users retagged other projects' instances with their own project value, and that some users changed the project tag on their own IAM users.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Deny ec2:CreateTags and ec2:DeleteTags for the project key, by using aws:TagKeys, to everyone except tag admins.",
      "Deny iam:TagUser and iam:UntagUser to the users, which stops them from changing their own project tags.",
      "Deny ec2:CreateTags for the project key only when aws:RequestTag/project equals the user's own project.",
      "Turn on AWS Config to record tag changes, and review the configuration history at the end of each month.",
      "Require MFA for ec2:CreateTags and iam:TagUser, and keep allowing users to change the project tag."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Users who can change the project tag on instances can move instances into their own project. Denying tag changes on that key protects the resource side of ABAC.",
      "Users who can change their own principal tags can join any project. Denying IAM tagging actions protects the principal side of ABAC.",
      "This stops only one value, and users could still remove the tag or set any other project.",
      "Monthly reviews find abuse after it has happened.",
      "MFA proves who the user is but still lets the user change the tags."] },

{ id: "d4-47", d: 4,
  q: "Users of an application sign in through an Amazon Cognito user pool, and an identity pool gives them AWS credentials. Each user has a custom:department attribute, and each Amazon S3 object has a department tag. IAM policies must allow access to an object only when its department tag matches the user's department, with one IAM role for all users.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on attributes for access control in the identity pool, map custom:department to a principal tag, and compare aws:PrincipalTag/department with s3:ExistingObjectTag/department.",
      "Turn on attributes for access control in the identity pool, map custom:department to a department principal tag, and compare aws:PrincipalTag/department with aws:ResourceTag/department.",
      "Create one identity pool and one IAM role for each department, and have the application send each user to the identity pool for the user's own department.",
      "Use the identity pool's default role for all users, and check each user's department attribute in the application before every request to Amazon S3."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attributes for access control turn user attributes into principal tags on the session. For S3 objects, the object's tag is read with s3:ExistingObjectTag, so one role's policy can compare the two.",
      "S3 object tags are not available through aws:ResourceTag, so the condition would not match objects.",
      "This uses a role for each department instead of one role for all users.",
      "Every user would hold credentials for all objects, and a check in the application can be bypassed."] },

{ id: "d4-48", d: 4,
  q: "IAM Access Analyzer reports public access findings for an Amazon S3 bucket that hosts the company's public website, and that access is intended. The security team wants findings for this bucket, and for future website buckets whose names start with www-, to be archived automatically. Findings for all other public buckets must stay active.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Access Analyzer archive rule for S3 bucket resources with public access whose resource ARN begins with arn:aws:s3:::www-.",
      "Create an Access Analyzer archive rule for S3 bucket resources with public access, with no other criteria in the rule.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the website bucket, which closes the finding and keeps the website online for users.",
      "Archive each finding by hand in the console when it appears, and leave the analyzer's archive rules unchanged."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Archive rules automatically archive new findings that match their criteria. Matching the resource ARN prefix archives findings for the website buckets only, so other public buckets keep active findings.",
      "Without the name criterion, the rule archives findings for every public bucket.",
      "Block Public Access stops public access, which takes the public website offline.",
      "Archiving by hand is manual work that an archive rule removes."] },

{ id: "d4-49", d: 4,
  q: "Employees federate to AWS through a SAML identity provider (IdP) and assume a shared role whose trust policy allows sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML and sts:TagSession. The role's policies use ABAC with aws:PrincipalTag/project. The employees can sign in, but every action on their project's resources is denied.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The IdP does not send the https://aws.amazon.com/SAML/Attributes/PrincipalTag:project attribute, so the session has no project tag.",
      "The role's trust policy does not allow sts:TagSession, so AWS drops the session tags that the IdP sends during federation.",
      "The role's ABAC policies must use aws:RequestTag/project for federated sessions instead of aws:PrincipalTag/project.",
      "ABAC conditions are not evaluated for sessions that are created with AssumeRoleWithSAML, only for IAM users."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session tags for SAML federation come from PrincipalTag attributes that the IdP sends. Without the project attribute, aws:PrincipalTag/project has no value, so every ABAC condition fails.",
      "The trust policy already allows sts:TagSession.",
      "Session tags are evaluated as principal tags, so aws:PrincipalTag is the right key for federated sessions.",
      "Principal tags work for federated sessions as well as for IAM users and roles."] },

{ id: "d4-50", d: 4,
  q: "A company attaches an identity policy to all users that denies all actions when aws:SourceIp is not in the corporate IP range. Since then, users in the office can no longer deploy AWS CloudFormation stacks, because the calls that CloudFormation makes to other services on their behalf are denied. Direct calls from outside the office must stay denied.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Add aws:ViaAWSService with a value of false to the conditions of the deny statement.",
      "Add aws:CalledVia with the value cloudformation.amazonaws.com to the conditions of the deny statement.",
      "Add the AWS IP address ranges of every Region to the allowed corporate ranges in the deny statement.",
      "Replace aws:SourceIp with aws:VpcSourceIp in the deny statement, and keep the corporate range in it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Calls that a service makes on a user's behalf come from the service's addresses. With aws:ViaAWSService false, the deny applies only to direct calls from users, so CloudFormation's calls work and direct calls from outside stay denied.",
      "With this condition, the deny would apply only to calls through CloudFormation, which blocks the deployments and lets direct calls from outside through.",
      "AWS ranges change often and are shared with other customers, so this weakens the control.",
      "aws:VpcSourceIp has a value only for requests through VPC endpoints, so the deny would stop working for internet requests."] },
]);
